# PRODUCT.md

# Product

<!-- impeccable:product-schema 1 -->

## Platform

adaptive

## Users

Trans people tracking their own gender transition day by day, on their own
device. The reader knows what dysphoria is, what a dose is, what a name
change costs; the app never explains their life to them. Some users open
the app in situations where being seen with it is unsafe, which is why
disguise mode and the app lock exist and gate what renders.

## Product Purpose

enGender keeps a person's gender transition in one place, on their own
device: entries carry mood, gender feelings on configurable scales, tags,
notes, photos, voice recordings and body-region feelings, plus milestones,
labs, regimen, measurements and the other care surfaces. There is no
account and no backend; data leaves the device only when the person
deliberately creates an archive. Success is the person seeing their own
history clearly, on their own terms.

## Positioning

One trans person wrote this for other trans people. GPLv3, no price, no
plan, nothing to upsell. Nothing in the app has to sell anything, and copy
written as though a company stood behind it reads as a lie. Local-first: no
account, no backend, data leaves the device only when the person
deliberately archives it - a neighbouring product with a business model
behind it could not truthfully copy that.

## Operating Context

SvelteKit PWA plus a Capacitor Android wrapper from one codebase, offline
first (one document, one cache per release, ADR-0021). English and Polish.
Colour is entirely data-attribute driven through a token layer
(`src/lib/theme/`), no utility framework, no CSS-in-JS; cards, buttons and
list rows are global classes, so restyling the app is a token edit. 68
routes exist. Navigation is four doors, named for what is behind them
rather than for what they show (ADR-0072): **Today** (`/`), **Journal**
(`/calendar`), **Look back** (`/stats`), and **Transition** (`/more`,
reads "More" under disguise), which holds every tracked area, add-first, in
four groups. Settings (`/settings`) is chrome under Today's own gear
(ADR-0076), not a fifth door, and holds only preferences plus the reference
areas no screen hosts inline. Feature surfaces that were once scattered
under `/settings/*` have their own door-prefixed addresses now
(`/care/*`, `/body/*`, `/health/*`, `/transition/*`, `/support/*`,
`/media/*`, `/voice`); every old `/settings/*` and `/practice/*` address
that moved still resolves in one hop as a redirect (ADR-0036). `/care` is
one medication module - a lane per running drug on its spine, with the
curve, the draw and the dose log linked from it rather than scattered
across separate top-level screens (phase 11 tickets 09-10). Every screen
lights a door in the bottom bar/rail (see
`src/lib/navigation/active-tab.ts`) -
except `/coming-back`, which is chromeless from redesign ticket 35 and so
has no bar to light: it is a step in DIRECTION.md rule 15's sense, one
purpose and no navigation, with the one way off in its own foot.
`active-tab.ts` maps it to the Today tab, and that mapping is load-bearing
rather than vestigial: it is what makes stepping into the moment from Home
a step inside one tab, which is what carries the field's blind (ADR-0080).

Android is the only native target; there is no iOS build and none is
planned, so where a reference offers both, Android's half is the one that
applies. Neither surface is a fallback for the other: the PWA is not a
degraded wrapper, and the wrapper is not a shell around a website.

The app owes Android its OS guarantees - the predictive back gesture,
window insets, a 48dp touch floor, the system's remove-animations setting -
and it takes Material 3's navigation and motion patterns mapped to its own
shape rather than applied by rote. It does not take Material's skin. Type
is bundled Nunito and Outfit, colour comes from the 8 flag palettes rather
than Material colour roles or Dynamic Color, and surfaces separate with a
line rather than tonal elevation. Those are identity decisions, not gaps to
close, and an audit that reads them as Material violations has misread the
product.

## Capabilities and Constraints

- 8 queer-flag palettes x light/dark carry identity - agender, bisexual,
  genderfluid, lesbian, nonbinary, pansexual, rainbow, trans. All 8 stay,
  whatever a redesign does with them. The flag needs no new assets:
  `--motif-stripes` lists in `src/lib/theme/palettes.css` are faithful
  stripe sequences per palette, including bisexual's doubled stops encoding
  its 2:1:2 proportions.
- Colour never judges (ADR-0012): single-hue intensity ramps, never
  red-to-green or diverging. Mood has its own fixed literal-hex scale
  (ADR-0025), independent of any palette.
- The pride motif is Today-only and never shows under disguise (ADR-0035,
  shipped).
- Android's launcher icon follows the chosen flag palette - one
  `activity-alias` per palette, the app's mark drawn in that palette's
  stripes. Disguise always outranks it and shows a neutral icon instead; a
  flag change alone never restarts the app. The web install's manifest icon
  stays fixed to the default palette, read once at install time; a running
  tab's favicon does follow (ADR-0088, shipped).
- Feature surfaces live behind the Transition door; Settings is chrome and
  holds only preferences plus the reference areas no screen hosts inline
  (ADR-0036, ADR-0076, ADR-0084, shipped).
- The front page is pinned: a person chooses what shows on Today - which
  areas, which agenda kinds, which live tiles - and the app ranks nothing
  centrally (ADR-0073, shipped).
- A dose schedule can log its own expected doses on the person's standing
  instruction, off by default; a dose written this way says so, can be
  marked skipped in one tap, and is marked in anything exported (ADR-0086,
  shipped).
- Every photograph the journal holds - entries, milestones, hair progress,
  tryouts, surgery - is browsed in one library with a source filter, not
  scattered across separate screens per owner (ADR-0085, shipped).
- No medical framing anywhere: the app never interprets a value or suggests
  a dose.
- One wrap system, four access modes - passphrase, PIN, biometric (web
  WebAuthn PRF), device-bound (Android Keystore, biometric-gated by the
  platform itself) - replace the old separate PIN gate (ADR-0041, shipped).
- Cycle tracking is not shown by default: the row and its prompts only
  appear for an active testosterone regimen or an explicit opt-in, so a
  transfemme reader is never asked a question the app already knows doesn't
  apply to her (ADR-0043, shipped).
- A full per-route inventory - what each screen must keep doing, where it's
  reachable from, and which of this repo's in-flight tickets change it -
  lives in `SCREENS.md`, not here. This file stays product truth; that file
  is the screen-by-screen brief.

## Brand Commitments

Voice per `docs/ui-copy.md`: second person, sentence case, British
spelling and variant A: friendly, conversational warmth with occasional light
humour in low-stakes invitations. Keep only explanations the person needs to act,
interpret a result or understand a consequence. Paper citations stay out of app
copy. Everyday tracking gets
factual feedback without praise or obligation; chosen milestones can be celebrated.
Polish conveys the meaning naturally, with current author vocabulary, idioms and
deliberate omissions intact. Neither language genders the reader. No emoji, no em
or en dashes, no "we". Type is bundled Nunito (body) and Outfit (display).
The app is named **enGender** (`messages/en.json`'s `app_name`;
`src/lib/disguise/identity.ts`'s `appWordmark()` is the only thing allowed
to render it, returning "Notes" under disguise) - the rename is decided,
not open.

## Evidence on Hand

Real UI strings in `messages/en.json` / `messages/pl.json`. The full domain
language lives in `CONTEXT.md`. Historical visual reference from the
project's original static mockup is in `mockup/DESIGN.md`; it predates the
More hub, current navigation, and the enGender rename, and should be read
as early-project context, not current truth - it still says "Gender
Diary" throughout, and any mock or prototype built from it has to swap in
the real name and header rather than copying its wordmark. The current,
authoritative screen-by-screen brief is `SCREENS.md`. Demo data renders
every screen without a journal (`VITE_DEMO=1`).

## Product Principles

- Private by default; anything visible to a bystander is a design decision.
- The person names their own identity; the app assumes nothing about
  direction or goal.
- Say what a screen does and get out of the way.
- No judgment encoded anywhere: not in colour, not in copy, not in a score.

## Accessibility & Inclusion

Floor from `docs/ui-copy.md` and the phase-5 UX spec: usable at 390px width
and 200% zoom, touch targets >= 48px (Android's floor, which is the stricter of the two platforms), WCAG 4.5:1 for body text (3:1 large),
the palette contrast test guards on-colour pairs across all 8 palettes x 2
themes.

